<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext posture.

  The SVCS server speaks plain HTTP with no TLS (docs/BLOCKERS.md records
  in-app HTTPS as a deliberate deferral in favor of a reverse proxy), so this
  app must permit cleartext to reach it at all. That is a real exposure, and
  the honest description of how it is contained is below.

  WHAT THE PLATFORM CANNOT DO, recorded so nobody spends a day re-attempting
  it: this file is a BUILD-TIME compiled resource. There is no runtime API to
  add a <domain> for the address the user just typed, and <domain> matches
  hostnames and literal IPs only, with no CIDR and no wildcards beyond one
  leading subdomain. RFC1918 is 17.9 million addresses. So the natural idea,
  "permit cleartext for exactly the user's own server and nothing else", is not
  expressible here at all.

  Attempting it anyway is worse than not trying: a base-config of
  cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" plus a handful of enumerated literals
  BLOCKS the primary use case, a server at 192.168.x.x, while looking strict.

  So the split is:
    * This file permits cleartext. It is the coarse switch and it cannot be
      more precise.
    * net/HostClassifier.kt is the ACTUAL gate. It refuses to send credentials
      to any address that is not loopback, RFC1918, RFC4193, link-local, or
      RFC6598 CGNAT (Tailscale), unless the user explicitly overrides for a
      public address. It mirrors is_private_bind() in src/gui/auth.py, and the
      Python side is tested in tests/test_network_guidance.py.

  If the server ever grows real TLS, flip base-config to false and delete this
  comment. That is the actual fix and it belongs on the server.

  Author: Bloodawn (KheivenD), 2026-07-18 (M1.1).
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
